## Branches and parallel work

Lane owns the PR; the **job** names the branch. Do not keep one standing branch per
agent and reuse it.

**Name:** `<lane>/<short-job>` — e.g. `doc/ask-clive-honesty`,
`kate/folio-source-pack-upload`, `ruth/aie-demo-retire`,
`clive-man/persona-config-resolve`.

**Rules:**

1. **One job → one branch → one PR → merge → stop.** Next job starts from fresh `main`.
2. The **owning lane** (who would be blamed if it’s wrong) opens and owns that PR.
3. Helpers may commit on the owner’s branch for the same job; they do **not** open a
   second long-lived branch for it. Split into two PRs only when the work truly separates.
4. Two jobs at once → two `<lane>/<job>` branches, ideally two worktrees. Never stack
   unrelated work onto a waiting or finished feature branch.
5. Before merge, refresh against `main` if other PRs landed. After merge, abandon the
   branch — do not continue living on it.
6. Mechanical grind (`Lane: none`) may use a short generic name (`fix/...`, `chore/...`)
   when a branch is needed; still one job per PR.

**New-chat brief (when starting work):** Goal · Lane · Branch from latest `main` ·
What not to touch · Done when · Session ID (or `none` if generic).

## Generic is legitimate

Mechanical repo grind stays **generic** (Composer / default agent). Emit `Lane: none`
and keep it inline — not Doc. Do not ceremony-route a two-line fix.

## When no lane clearly owns it

Use **generic**, tell Matthew which lane you considered and why it did not fit, and
proceed. Do not stall for a perfect owner.

## Multi-lane requests

Most big requests touch several lanes. **Do not ask which one.** Dispatch the lane that
owns the **next** step, and let that agent route onward — every household agent carries
`household-routing-standard`.

Ordering when it is unclear:

1. Decision not yet made → `clive`
2. Decision made, needs challenge and is Red plus novel → `pam`
3. Decision made, needs building → `doc` (or `kate` for scenic, `ruth-hadley` for schema)
4. Something durable happened → `clive-man`

Ask a clarifying question only when the lane is genuinely ambiguous **and** guessing
wrong would waste real work. One question, naming both candidates.

## Model discipline

Dispatch does not override `.cursor/rules/model-routing.mdc`. Each agent carries its own
pin. When frontier is in **drought** (credits empty), still dispatch the named agent;
pass Grok 4.6 for reasoning heads and Composer for hands — see that rule's drought
section. When you keep work inline, the same rule applies: mechanical edits belong on a
first-party-pool model, never a frontier one.

## Known limitation

This rule cannot reorder or filter Cursor's `@` picker UI. It removes the need to use
the picker; it does not fix it.
